#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L01_equiv
msgid "## **Level 1**\n"
"We've unlocked two theorems for this first level, namely: §0 and §1. 🔍 Check out the entries for these theorems in the **Theorems** tab.\n"
"\n"
"The conditional law says that §2 is logically equivalent to §3. This looks *very* close to that statement. We might start by ***rewriting*** the right-hand side of our goal...\n"
"  ### **❯ The §4 tactic**\n"
"  In order to apply ***rewrites*** (or substitutions) in Lean, we use the §5 tactic. 🔍 Check out the entry for §6 in the **Tactics** tab for details. 👉 In this specific case, try\n"
"  §7\n"
"  This will apply the **Commutative Law** for §8 at the first instance in the goal where it is applicable (namely, the right-hand side in this case)."
msgstr ""

#. §0: ```
#. rewrite[Or_imp]
#. ```
#. §1: `Or_imp`
#. §2: ```
#. Or_imp (P Q : Prop) : (¬ P ∨ Q) ↔ (P → Q)
#. ```
#. §3: `P → Q`
#. §4: `¬ P ∨ Q`
#. §5: ```
#. rewrite[← Or_imp]
#. ```
#. §6: `←`
#. §7: `\\left`
#. §8: `<-`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L02_equiv
msgid "Maybe we tried\n"
"§0\n"
"and it didn't work. Why? Check the entry for §1 in the **Theorems** tab. Note that\n"
"§2\n"
"We want to apply this law ***in reverse***. In other words, we want to rewrite §3 to §4. 👉 In order to do this, try the syntax:\n"
"§5\n"
"### **⌨ Typesetting Tip**\n"
"In order to get the pretty printed left arrow §6 use §7. You can also use §8 instead."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `Or_imp`
#. §1: ```
#. rw[← Or_imp,← Or_imp]
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L03_equiv
msgid "That was a good first step. You might want to apply the **Conditional Law** codified in §0 two more times. You can do this in one step with:\n"
"§1\n"
""
msgstr ""

